  build: {
    minify: 'esbuild',
    terserOptions: { format: { comments: /^!/ } } // only if using terser
  }
}
```

If this is a genuine regression, please share a minimal repro and the exact Vite version—otherwise I'd recommend filing it at github.com/vitejs/vite/issues.

This is a known behavior difference: Rolldown's parser/transform pipeline can strip or fail to preserve `/* @vite-ignore */` comments attached to dynamic `import()` calls, so Vite's `vite:dynamic-import-vars` / import-analysis plugin no longer sees the hint and either warns or tries to analyze the specifier.

Workarounds:

```js
// Keep the comment on the same line, immediately before the argument
const mod = await import(/* @vite-ignore */ url)

// Or bypass analysis entirely
const mod = await import(/* @vite-ignore */ `${url}`)
```

If it still gets dropped, check your `vite`/`rolldown-vite` version — this has been reported and fixed in several releases, so upgrading is the first thing to try. If you're on the latest and it persists, it's worth filing an issue with a minimal repro, since comment preservation across the Rolldown transform is something they've been actively patching. I'm not certain of the exact version where it was resolved, so verify against the changelog.

Vite's dependency optimizer writes its cache to `node_modules/.vite` by default. You can relocate it with the `cacheDir` option:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  cacheDir: './.vite-cache', // relative to project root
}
```

If you're seeing stale or corrupted optimizer output, the usual fix is to delete the cache directory (or run `vite --force` / `optimizeDeps.force: true`) rather than moving it. Note that `cacheDir` must be inside the project root for Vite to manage it correctly. If your issue is specifically about a lock file (e.g., `package-lock.json` or a `.lock` file in the cache dir), Vite doesn't expose a config for that location—it's derived from `cacheDir`.

Vite's dev server only watches and HMRs files it knows about — PHP files aren't part of the module graph, so changes to them won't trigger a reload by default. The usual fix is to add them to `server.watch` and trigger a full reload via a plugin:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  plugins: [{
    name: 'php-reload',
    handleHotUpdate({ file, server }) {
      if (file.endsWith('.php')) {
        server.ws.send({ type: 'full-reload' })
        return []
      }
    }
  }],
  server: {
    watch: { ignored: ['**/.git/**'] } // ensure PHP isn't ignored
  }
}
```

If Tailwind's content scanning is the issue (styles not updating), make sure your `@source`/`content` globs include the PHP files. Note that Vite 7.1 changed some watcher defaults (e.g. `server.watch` behavior and ignored patterns), so explicitly listing PHP extensions or removing broad `ignored` globs often restores the old behavior. If you're using a backend integration plugin (e.g. `vite-plugin-php` or Laravel's Vite plugin), check its version compatibility with 7.1 — that's the most common culprit.

In Vite's dev server, `server.proxy` uses `http-proxy`, so you can set upstream basic auth via `proxyReq` and forward the client's `Authorization` header separately. Note that `http-proxy` strips the `Authorization` header by default when `changeOrigin` is used, so re-add it explicitly.

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  server: {
    proxy: {
      '/api': {
        target: 'https://upstream.example.com',
        changeOrigin: true,
        configure: (proxy) => {
          proxy.on('proxyReq', (proxyReq, req) => {
            // Upstream basic auth
            const creds = Buffer.from('user:pass').toString('base64')
            proxyReq.setHeader('Authorization', `Basic ${creds}`)
            // Forward client's own auth header under a different name if needed
            if (req.headers['x-client-auth']) {
              proxyReq.setHeader('X-Client-Authorization', req.headers['x-client-auth'])
            }
          })
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

